WEBVTT

NOTE Hit Your Mark introduction. English captions.

1
00:00:00.330 --> 00:00:02.980
Three. Two. One. Hit your mark.

2
00:00:03.510 --> 00:00:07.320
On stage, a performer walks
toward a tape mark on the floor.

3
00:00:07.650 --> 00:00:12.000
Every second, the distance to the
mark drops three quarters of a meter.

4
00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:16.370
In the pool, a swimmer pushes
off the wall and glides.

5
00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:20.810
Every second, the distance from the
start grows one and a half meters.

6
00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:23.530
One number goes down. One goes up.

7
00:00:23.810 --> 00:00:25.380
But both of them are lines.

8
00:00:25.910 --> 00:00:29.740
The slope is the rate: how much
the distance changes each second.

9
00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:34.970
The y-intercept is where the line
crosses the y-axis, at zero seconds.

10
00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:39.640
Six meters from the mark on stage.
Three meters from the start in the pool.

11
00:00:39.970 --> 00:00:41.900
That's where you are
when the clock starts.

12
00:00:42.380 --> 00:00:45.330
Change either one, and
the whole line moves.

13
00:00:45.860 --> 00:00:47.610
Now, here's the problem.

14
00:00:47.990 --> 00:00:49.840
The director moves the cue earlier.

15
00:00:50.170 --> 00:00:51.800
The coach wants a faster split.

16
00:00:52.180 --> 00:00:54.270
Keep the old plan, and you miss.

17
00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:58.110
So what do you change:
the rate, or the start?

18
00:00:58.540 --> 00:01:03.370
Choose your scene. Build the model.
Graph it, adjust it, and hit your mark.
